  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nUpper-level ridging stretching from the Great Lakes up past Hudson \nBay will continue to slide east early this week. Meanwhile, the low-\nlevel jet extending from the southern Great Plains into the Midwest \nwill intensify later tonight into Monday morning, providing \nforcing/warm air advection over a slow-moving warm front/stationary \nfront draped across the region. A seasonably strong shortwave looks \nto progress across south-central Canada and the northern Great Lakes \nin the Tuesday-Wednesday timeframe as aforementioned ridging works \neast. Expansive, amplified ridging then looks to build across the \ncenter of the continent late this week and into this upcoming \nweekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nPrimary concern will be the increasing potential for localized heavy \nrainfall and flooding -- especially Monday morning. Unfortunately, \nlower-than-usual confidence currently exists in the evolution of \nshowers and storms this evening through Monday. Latest forecast \nthoughts are that at least some additional showers and storms will \nform this afternoon and evening across the southeast quadrant of our \nCWA and eventually move out over Lake Huron. A relative break in \nrain chances aside from a few stray showers is anticipated late this \nevening/early tonight before the heaviest rain potential moves in \nearly Monday morning. \n\nAforementioned low-level jet forcing is currently expected to kick \noff showers and storms during this time, but precise location and \ntrack of this activity is still highly uncertain. Based on latest \nobservations and guidance, the best chance for training showers and \nstorms through Monday morning will come across the Straits and \nnorthern lower Michigan along and north of M-32. Should heavy \nshowers/strong storms materialize, an impressively moist environment \nwill be in place to support torrential rainfall Monday morning -- \nwhich is the primary source of concern analyzing latest guidance \ndespite relatively low confidence in how showers/storms will evolve. \nWith PWATs of 1.8\"+ exceeding the max moving average and potentially \ndaily max values this evening/Monday morning, any organized \nshowers/storms will be capable of producing rainfall rates well in \nexcess of 1\" per hour. Should activity train along/north of the \nboundary for a few hours, rainfall totals of 2-4\" would be possible \nby Monday afternoon -- along with low chances for 4\"+ if strong \nstorms track over certain areas. While severe storms appear unlikely \nat this time, small hail and gusty winds cannot be ruled out given \nat least some elevated buoyancy combined with strong deep-layer \nshear. It is emphasized that high uncertainty still exists with a \nwide range of potential forecast solutions for Monday morning, \nspanning from little rainfall if showers/storms struggle to form to \nthe high-end amounts mentioned above. For additional details on \npotential impacts, please see the Hydrology Discussion below.\n\nLooking ahead, rain coverage is expected to taper through Monday \nevening. Slight chances for additional showers/storms will be in \nplace into the middle of the week with much of that time and most \nlocations remaining dry. Temperatures will warm over the next few \ndays with highs potentially reaching into the mid to upper 80s on \nTuesday. Mostly dry weather is expected by the end of the week and \ninto the holiday weekend as previously mentioned ridging helps build \nhigh pressure into the region.",
